sum='In this short free-verse poem, a child describes evening play. Every evening the children play ball beside a creek (a small stream) in their neighbour’s field. They run so fast that the speaker is completely out of breath – the poet writes these words one below the other to show the panting. As the dark blue evening sky turns black, the grass becomes cold and makes their feet ache, and the trees seem to creep closer in the darkness. The game is over – the night has “won”. The poet gives night a human quality (personification) – night is the winner because it ends the game. The poet, Georgia Heard, is an American poet and teacher who received the 2023 Excellence in Poetry for Children Award.',
